$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: General Procedure for β-Iodoallenolate
Cyclizations Run with MgI2. Magnesium iodide (at the indicated
equivalents) was added to a stirred solution of the alkynone (1.0 equiv)
in dry CH2Cl2 (0.10 M) or THF (0.10 M) at 0 °C. The reaction was then
carried out at the indicated temperature and time. After completion of
the reaction, the mixture was diluted with ethyl acetate, quenched with
saturated NaHCO3 solution, and extracted with ethyl acetate (3×). The
combined organic layers were washed with saturated Na2S2O3 solution (2×)
and brine (1×), dried over MgSO4, and concentrated. The resulting residue
was purified by flash chromatography on silica gel using different
gradients of hexanes and ethyl acetate. The pure products.
(1R,3R,3aS,6aR,Z)-1-Hydroxy-3,3a,6,9-tetramethyl-2,3,3a,4,7,8-hexahydro-1H-cycloocta[de]naphthalen-11(6aH)-one
(8a) and (3aS,4R,6R,11aR,Z)-6-Hydroxy-3a,4,9-trimethyl-1-
methylene-2,3,3a,4,5,6,11,11a-octahydro-1H-cycloocta[de]-naphthalen-7(10H)-one
(8b). Compounds 8a (endo) and 8b (exo) were obtained in a 2.7:1 ratio.
Yield: 10 mg, 23%. Eluent: hexanes/ethyl acetate, 80:20.
1-(6-Hydroxy-2-iodo-3,3-dimethylcyclohex-1-en-1-yl)-3-methylbut-2-en-1-one
(11). Compound 11 was prepared from alkynone 10 using the general
protocol for the MgI2-promoted cyclization. Yield: 526 mg, 82%. Eluent:
hexanes:ethyl acetate, 80:20). 1H NMR (500 MHz, CDCl3): δ 6.27 (s, 1H),
4.34 (m, 1H), 2.62 (s, 1H), 2.24 (s, 3H), 2.00 (s, 3H), 2.06-1.93 (m,
1H), 1.85-1.83 (m, 1H), 1.78-1.74 (m, 1H), 1.20 (s, 3H), 1.12 (s, 3H).
13C NMR (125 MHz, CDCl3): δ 197.5, 158.4, 147.3, 123.7, 118.8, 68.0,
39.1, 32.6, 31.5, 29.0, 27.8, 21.3. IR (neat): 3623-3095, 2962, 2930,
2860, 1664, 1603. HRMS (ESI-TOF): m/z calcd for C13H19O2I1Na [M+Na+]
357.0321, found 357.0322.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM optimization study (optimized on lewis acid used, iodide, time and
temperature)
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Cascade Cyclizations of Acyclic and Macrocyclic Alkynones: Studies toward
the Synthesis of Phomactin A
